\subsubsection{Hybrid, iterative, and structured retrieval}

The preceding retrieval paradigms are not mutually exclusive. Hybrid retrieval systems combine lexical and neural signals, for example by merging or reranking results produced by sparse and dense retrievers. Such combinations seek to benefit simultaneously from the precision of exact lexical matching and the semantic generalization offered by learned representations.

Retrieval can also extend beyond a single query--ranking operation. Iterative approaches perform several retrieval steps, allowing information obtained at one stage to influence subsequent searches. This is particularly relevant when answering questions whose required evidence cannot be identified effectively from the original query alone.

Other approaches impose additional structure on the retrieval space. Graph-based retrieval systems represent entities, passages, facts, or their relationships as nodes and edges, enabling retrieval to exploit connections between pieces of information rather than treating every passage independently~\cite{gutierrez2024hipporag}. Such architectures are particularly attractive for multi-hop reasoning, where the evidence required to answer a question may form a chain of related information distributed across different documents.

These extensions illustrate that the retriever used in a RAG system need not be restricted to a particular architecture. The research developed later in this thesis therefore treats retrieval primarily through its observable output---a ranked set of candidate evidence passages---rather than assuming a single retrieval mechanism. This makes it possible to reason about retrieval quality across sparse, neural, late-interaction, and more structured retrieval systems.

Regardless of the retrieval architecture, the output can ultimately be represented as an ordered list
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:ranked-list}
  R(q)=\left[d_{(1)},d_{(2)},\ldots,d_{(N)}\right],
\end{equation}
where \(d_{(1)}\) is the passage assigned the highest relevance to \(q\), \(d_{(2)}\) the second highest, and so forth. The parenthesised subscript denotes a position in this ranking and not an identifier of the document within \(\mathcal{C}\); the top-\(k\) set of Equation~\eqref{eq:retrieval} is accordingly the length-\(k\) prefix of \(R(q)\). For question answering, an important issue is therefore not only whether the supporting evidence exists somewhere in the corpus, but also where that evidence appears in the ranking produced for the query.

This observation motivates the notions of retrieval rank, top-\(k\) retrieval, and evidence coverage introduced in the following subsection.

\subsection{Retrieval Ranking, Top-\texorpdfstring{\(k\)}{k}, and Evidence Coverage}
\label{subsec:retrieval-ranking}

The ranking \(R(q)\) of Equation~\eqref{eq:ranked-list} is particularly important in Retrieval-Augmented Generation because only a limited number of retrieved passages are typically included in the context presented to the language model. Consequently, the position at which a relevant passage appears directly determines whether it is available to the downstream generation stage for a given retrieval budget.

For a particular evidence passage \(e\), its retrieval rank with respect to the query \(q\) is defined as
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:evidence-rank}
  \operatorname{rank}(e\mid q)=i \quad\text{if}\quad e=d_{(i)}.
\end{equation}
A smaller value of \(\operatorname{rank}(e\mid q)\) therefore indicates that the evidence is retrieved earlier and can be accessed using a smaller retrieval budget.

In practical RAG systems, the entire ranked corpus is not supplied to the language model. Instead, only the first \(k\) retrieved passages are retained:
\[
  D_k(q)=\left\{d_{(1)},d_{(2)},\ldots,d_{(k)}\right\}.
\]
This operation is commonly referred to as top-\(k\) retrieval. A passage \(e\) is available within the retrieved context at depth \(k\) if
\[
  \operatorname{rank}(e\mid q)\leq k.
\]
The value of \(k\) therefore plays an important role in the interaction between retrieval and generation. A small value limits the amount of context that must be processed by the language model and reduces the number of potentially distracting passages. However, if relevant evidence is ranked below this threshold, it will not be available to the generator. Increasing \(k\) improves the opportunity to recover lower-ranked evidence, but also increases the amount of retrieved material that must be processed.

\subsubsection{Supporting evidence}

For question-answering tasks, relevance is ultimately determined by whether the retrieved passages contain the information required to derive the answer. Let
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:evidence-set}
  E(q)=\left\{e_1,e_2,\ldots,e_m\right\}
\end{equation}
denote the set of supporting evidence passages required for question \(q\). In a single-hop question, this set may contain only one principal supporting passage. In multi-hop question answering, by contrast, several evidence passages may be necessary because different parts of the reasoning chain can depend on information distributed across multiple documents.

A retrieved passage is therefore useful not merely because it is semantically related to the query, but because it contributes evidence required by the reasoning process. This distinction is important: a retriever may return many passages that are topically relevant while still failing to retrieve one of the specific pieces of evidence needed to answer the question.

The relationship between supporting evidence and the top-\(k\) retrieved set can be expressed through evidence recall at depth \(k\):
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:evidence-recall}
  \operatorname{EvidenceRecall}@k=\frac{\left|E(q)\cap D_k(q)\right|}{|E(q)|}.
\end{equation}
This quantity measures the proportion of required evidence passages that occur within the first \(k\) retrieval results. A value of \(1\) indicates that all annotated supporting evidence has been recovered, whereas a value below \(1\) indicates that at least one required passage remains outside the retrieved context.

For questions requiring multiple evidence passages, it is also useful to distinguish partial evidence retrieval from complete evidence coverage. Complete evidence coverage at depth \(k\) occurs when
\begin{equation}
  \label{eq:complete-coverage}
  E(q)\subseteq D_k(q).
\end{equation}
Equivalently, every required evidence passage must satisfy
\[
  \operatorname{rank}(e\mid q)\leq k,\qquad \forall e\in E(q).
\]
This distinction is especially important in multi-hop settings. Recovering most of the supporting evidence may still be insufficient if one missing passage corresponds to an essential reasoning step. For example, if a question requires three pieces of evidence appearing at ranks \(2\), \(4\), and \(18\), as illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:evidence-ranking-example}, then retrieval with \(k=5\) recovers two thirds of the annotated evidence but does not provide complete evidence coverage. The final passage only becomes available once the retrieval depth reaches at least \(18\).

