\subsubsection{Retrieval depth and context size}

The retrieval depth \(k\) can be interpreted as a computational and informational budget allocated to retrieval. At low values of \(k\), the language model receives a compact context composed of the passages considered most relevant by the retriever. At larger values, the probability of recovering evidence located deeper in the ranking can increase, but the context simultaneously becomes larger and potentially more difficult to exploit.

This trade-off is particularly significant for multi-hop questions. If different pieces of required evidence are ranked at substantially different positions, obtaining complete evidence coverage may require retrieving many passages even when some supporting evidence is already ranked near the top. The language model must then identify a comparatively small number of useful passages among a larger collection of retrieved material and combine them correctly.

Retrieval quality in RAG should therefore not be reduced to whether at least one relevant passage appears near the top of the ranking. For questions involving several reasoning steps, the placement of \emph{all} required evidence is relevant. The ranking produced by the retriever determines both whether this evidence is accessible under a given top-\(k\) budget and how much additional context must be introduced before complete evidence coverage is obtained.

These concepts provide the retrieval-level foundation used throughout the remainder of this thesis. In particular, the rank of individual evidence passages and their joint coverage within the retrieval ranking will later provide a means of comparing the retrieval demands of an original multi-hop question with those of queries obtained through question decomposition. The specific retrieval-depth measures developed for this comparison are introduced in Chapter~\ref{chap:empirical-studies}, after the decomposition problem has been formally defined.

